This Python implementation is maintained separately from the original gRodon R
package. It is not an official release of the upstream authors.

Scientific algorithms and fitted model parameters originate in gRodon 2.7.3,
by JL Weissman and contributors, source revision
4b13053e7f49ca99bceeee36940f5e820c5b0c32:
https://github.com/jlw-ecoevo/gRodon2

Codon statistics follow the coRdon definitions by Anamaria Elek, Maja Kuzman,
and Kristian Vlahovicek. This package implements the numerical formulas in
Python; it does not bundle the coRdon R code.
https://bioconductor.org/packages/coRdon

The repository's MIT LICENSE applies to the Python implementation. The upstream
R DESCRIPTION contains the literal License field "use_mit_license()" and the
source checkout has no accompanying license file. This does not establish an
unambiguous redistribution license for the fitted model data. The data retain
upstream attribution and provenance; obtain clarification from the original
authors before publicly redistributing those assets.

The optional genome pipeline includes a derived bacterial ribosomal reference
snapshot from UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot release 2026_03, The UniProt Consortium.
The source query selects reviewed bacterial proteins carrying keyword KW-0689.
The export retains complete canonical structural ribosomal proteins, excludes
fragments and associated/unsupported entries, sorts identifiers, normalizes
FASTA wrapping, and compresses the result. Full source identifiers and protein
descriptions are preserved. Source URL, release, retrieval date, selection
rules, and checksums are recorded in:
src/grodon/data/references/bacteria-swissprot-v1.json

UniProt applies the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International license
(CC BY 4.0) to copyrightable parts of its databases. Credit The UniProt
Consortium and identify the source release and these export modifications when
redistributing this reference snapshot.
https://www.uniprot.org/help/license
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
https://www.uniprot.org/citations

The lactococcus-v1 reference is an archived selection of NCBI RefSeq protein
records from GCF_000009425.1, GCF_000006865.1, and GCF_964062975.1. Attribution
belongs to NCBI RefSeq and the contributing genome projects. Source URLs,
accessions, annotations, and checksums are preserved in:
src/grodon/data/references/lactococcus-v1.json
NCBI makes sequence data available for public use and notes that submitters
may assert intellectual-property rights; its policy is available at:
https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/home/about/policies/

Pyrodigal and NCBI BLAST+ are separately installed optional tools. Their
upstream licenses and citations continue to apply. BLAST+ binaries are not
bundled in the Python distribution; the explicit setup command retrieves an
official pinned NCBI release into the user's chosen local directory.
